WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:09.900
Investigators, journalists and lawyers share one problem: too much evidence to read, and a result nobody can later prove was not changed.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:12.490
Sealbook works in three steps.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.580
You bring the material in.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:21.180
The app reads all of it and proposes connections, each with the exact place it came from.

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:28.330
A person approves or declines each one with a signature, and the record is sealed, so anyone can check it later.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.760
Four invented cases show how that helps.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:40.850
One: a freight company pays a supplier, a consultant, a guarantor and a ship agent. Four desks, four clean files.

00:00:41.450 --> 00:00:50.300
Are they four people, or one man under four names? The desks hold 14 sources and 1,445 rows.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:57.340
Kassar. Qassar. Khassar. Kasar. And on one licence, the name in Arabic.

00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:04.940
Four spellings, two alphabets, one man. And one who is not him: a surveyor with the same name, born elsewhere.

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:14.800
The app proposed each link with the passage behind it. Fixed rules, with no AI model, found 6 of the 6 true links, and offered the surveyor too.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:21.710
A person declines the surveyor, with a reason, and signs each decision with a passkey on their own device.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:29.060
The case closes on a conclusion, signed by a person and checked by a colleague: one man, under four names.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:38.200
Two: a workshop burns down at night. The bookkeeper says she spent the evening at her sister's, 34 kilometres away.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.160
Was she? There are 41 statements to read.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:51.650
The app reads every paper itself. On this scanned exhibit sheet, it reads the page, then pulls out who was where, boxed where it is written.

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:57.140
It describes the photograph from her phone, and writes down the voice note she sent.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:08.180
Then it flags what does not fit: those two exhibits place her at the workshop at 21:52 and 22:07, while she says she was away from six.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:16.850
A person checks both against her statement and signs: she was at the yard on Tanner's Row that evening, not in Brindle Cross from six.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.040
Three: a flood video spreads as breaking news.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.710
Is it new? The tip line holds 36 clips that look alike.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:36.550
The app compares their sound and ties the viral clip to a video posted two years earlier. It had been cropped and mirrored, so the picture no longer matched.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:44.720
8 of 9 reposts were tied this way; one muted copy is missed. A person signs that the clip is old.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.540
Four: a nurse boards a night bus and is never seen again.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.380
Where do you look? The bus company's logs run to several hundred lines.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:05.810
A supervisor's statement says when the driver was last seen. From that night, the app finds 73 days in which no log names him.

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:17.490
The conclusion, signed and checked by a colleague: Aron Vesk is named in no log for the next 73 days; this is a lead to follow, not a finding about what he did.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:29.540
Every decision is signed by a person, and the case is sealed. Here the first case's real package is checked in a browser, with nothing uploaded.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:36.800
The machine reads more than a person can, and finds the links. A person approves every one.
